### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer is evaluating leadership, communication, and your ability to multiply your impact through others. They want evidence that you can grow a less-experienced engineer and improve the team's overall capability.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class MentoringTest {
    enum Signal { MULTIPLIER, SOLO }
    public static Signal evaluate(boolean taughtThinking, boolean juniorGrewIndependent) {
        return (taughtThinking && juniorGrewIndependent) ? Signal.MULTIPLIER : Signal.SOLO;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true)); // MULTIPLIER
    }
}
```

### What was the specific gap?

A junior developer at National Bank of India used Java's double type for the monthly interest calculation on savings accounts. The tiny floating-point rounding errors added up across thousands of accounts and broke end-of-month reconciliation.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
public class DoubleProblem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Wrong: double for money
        double balanceDouble = 280000.0;
        double rateDouble = 0.04 / 12;
        double interestDouble = balanceDouble * rateDouble;
        System.out.println("double interest: " + interestDouble); // imprecise

        // Correct: BigDecimal for money
        BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("280000.00");
        BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("0.04").divide(new BigDecimal("12"), 10, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);
        BigDecimal interest = balance.multiply(rate).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);
        System.out.println("BigDecimal interest: " + interest); // exact
    }
}
```

```text
double interest: 933.3333333333334
BigDecimal interest: 933.33
```

### How did you teach the principle, not just the rule?

I explained the why: money needs exact decimal arithmetic because tiny rounding errors compound across thousands of accounts. "Use BigDecimal" is a rule the junior forgets; "money needs exact arithmetic" is a principle they apply everywhere.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class PrincipleVsRule {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Rule: 'Use BigDecimal for money' -> forgets in 2 weeks");
        System.out.println("Principle: 'Money needs exact decimal arithmetic because "
            + "tiny errors compound across 100K accounts' -> applies everywhere");
    }
}
```

### How did you show the error concretely?

I ran the double calculation next to the BigDecimal calculation on Rajesh's and Priya's balances. The junior could see with their own eyes that double produced rounding noise.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
public class SideBySideDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double rajeshDouble = 280000.0 * (0.04 / 12);
        BigDecimal rajeshBD = new BigDecimal("280000.00")
            .multiply(new BigDecimal("0.04").divide(new BigDecimal("12"), 10, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN))
            .setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);
        System.out.println("Rajesh (double):     " + rajeshDouble);
        System.out.println("Rajesh (BigDecimal): " + rajeshBD);
        System.out.println("The difference is tiny per account but catastrophic across 100K accounts.");
    }
}
```

### How did you let the junior drive the fix?

I let the junior rewrite the interest calculation using BigDecimal while I reviewed. Teaching is not doing it for them; it is standing beside them while they do it.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class JuniorDrives {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Step 1: I explained the principle and showed the error");
        System.out.println("Step 2: Junior rewrote the calculation using BigDecimal");
        System.out.println("Step 3: I reviewed and gave feedback on scale and rounding mode");
        System.out.println("Step 4: Junior submitted the fix with confidence");
    }
}
```

### How did you create psychological safety?

I shared my own past mistake with double arithmetic so the junior knew this was a common trap, not a personal failing. Normalizing mistakes makes juniors ask questions earlier.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class PsychologicalSafety {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("I said: 'I made the exact same mistake early in my career.'");
        System.out.println("Effect: junior stopped hiding confusion and started asking earlier.");
        System.out.println("Result: fewer bugs caught late, more bugs caught at review time.");
    }
}
```

### How did you measure success?

The junior caught two other places in the codebase where double was being used for money before I even reviewed them. That independent detection is the proof of successful mentoring.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class MentoringSuccess {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Before mentoring: junior used double for money without question");
        System.out.println("After mentoring: junior independently found 2 more double-money bugs");
        System.out.println("Measurement: the junior can now catch this class of issue without me");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "How did you handle it if the junior pushed back?" (use data, not authority), "Did you mentor anyone else?" (show a pattern), and "What did you learn from mentoring?" (teaching forces you to clarify your own understanding).

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class MentoringFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pushback? -> Showed the numbers; data convinced them");
        System.out.println("Others? -> Yes, I mentor 1-2 juniors per quarter");
        System.out.println("What I learned? -> Teaching clarifies my own understanding");
    }
}
```
